WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:05.630
The incredibly rich fantasy world of Onward
is positively bursting with easter eggs and

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:10.931
amazing hidden details from deep-cut Toy Story
and Pixar references to secret Marvel Infinity

00:00:10.931 --> 00:00:11.931
Gauntlets.

00:00:11.931 --> 00:00:15.960
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'll be revealing over 50 of the

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.340
very best easter eggs and hidden details to
look out for in Onward.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:24.510
There'll be a few mild spoilers ahead, but
I'll avoid any major plot reveals.

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:28.840
Pixar's dedication to recurring easter eggs
has fuelled the idea that their movies exist

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:32.750
in a single shared universe and there's a
fascinating connection between Onward and

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:36.170
Brave that fans of the Pixar theory will love.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:40.010
When Barley and Ian are looking at the map
for how to get to Raven's Point, there's an

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:44.260
area called "The Brave Wilderness" which is
also next to "Loch Lake".

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:48.340
Brave is set in the Scottish Highlands and
during the brothers' trip they pass by some

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:52.120
ruined stone buildings that fit the scenery
of parts of rural Scotland.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:57.000
There's even some tall stones which have fallen
over and could possibly have been part of

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.830
an old ring of stones, similar to where Merida
sees the wisps for the first time.

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:06.009
Another Pixar universe connection in Onward
is the appearance of TripleDent gum.

00:01:06.009 --> 00:01:10.759
You can spot the Pixie Dusters grabbing it
at the gas station and Ian also has a packet

00:01:10.759 --> 00:01:13.200
underneath his old tape player at home.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.469
TripleDent Gum is of course the chewing gum
brand from Inside Out with that very memorable

00:01:17.469 --> 00:01:19.640
jingle Riley couldn't get out of her head.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.020
"TripleDent gum..
..will make you smile."

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:24.570
"Did I ask for the gum commercial?!"

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:29.709
Also in the gas station is a very sneaky Toy
Story reference via a tiny little cup with

00:01:29.709 --> 00:01:31.539
pens on the cashier's counter.

00:01:31.539 --> 00:01:36.179
The chicken logo is from Poultry Palace, the
fast-food restaurant that Bonnie and her family

00:01:36.179 --> 00:01:39.409
visited on their road trip in Toy Story 4.

00:01:39.409 --> 00:01:43.049
Poultry Palace first appeared in the Toy Story
short film "Small Fry".

00:01:43.049 --> 00:01:47.709
And there was also a vintage "Ye Olde Poultry
Palace" sign in the antiques store in Toy

00:01:47.709 --> 00:01:48.920
Story 4.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:53.090
By the way, if you missed it at the time,
Toy Story 4 had an easter egg for Onward in

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:54.569
one of its mid-credit scenes.

00:01:54.569 --> 00:01:58.729
The winged Pegasus design on a bouncy castle
at the carnival is the same one as on the

00:01:58.729 --> 00:02:00.819
side of Barley's van, Guinevere.

00:02:00.819 --> 00:02:05.579
The Pizza Planet truck from Pixar's first
Toy Story movie which has appeared in almost

00:02:05.579 --> 00:02:09.530
every Pixar film so far, is referenced several
ways in Onward.

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:14.000
Like in Coco, where the truck was rebranded
as "Pizza Planeta", in Onward the name has

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:19.000
been changed to "Pizza Realm", fitting better
with the film's olde worlde sensibilities.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.530
The "Pizza Realm" design still has the iconic
planet, however, a mast and banner have also

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:26.530
been added and the font has been given a vintage
look.

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:31.040
You can also spot a Pizza Realm truck queuing
in the traffic at the toll booth as Barley

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:32.959
and Ian begin their quest.

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:37.750
Also, watch out for the cooler in the back
of Barley's van as it has a Pizza Realm sticker

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:42.599
on it, though it's hard to spot as the box
moves around a lot as they drive.

00:02:42.599 --> 00:02:46.959
In keeping with its themes of wizardry and
spell casting, Onward pays homage to a number

00:02:46.959 --> 00:02:49.560
of other magical fantasy Disney movies.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:53.810
Some of the staff at the Manticore's tavern
wear hats that are very similar to the Sorceror's

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:57.019
Hat which Mickey Mouse first wore in Disney's
Fantasia.

00:02:57.019 --> 00:03:01.690
The Sorceror's hat is iconic in Disney history
and has appeared in many other Disney productions

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:03.890
from Wreck-It Ralph 2 to Once Upon A Time.

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:08.370
There's a store called "Sword in the Scone"
in Onward, which is a nod to Disney's animated

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:10.629
fantasy movie "The Sword in the Stone".

00:03:10.629 --> 00:03:14.659
And that pun is a double easter egg because
"Sword in the Scone" is also the name of the

00:03:14.659 --> 00:03:19.170
breakfast sandwich on the menu at Poultry
Palace in Toy Story 4 and Small Fry.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:23.900
Look carefully when Ian walks in to the gas
station because there are "Magic Beans" on

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:27.680
sale, a reference to the fairy tale of Jack
and the Beanstalk.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.239
And in Onward, as Ian and Barley go on their
quest, they drive right through fields filled

00:03:32.239 --> 00:03:35.790
with giant beanstalks that stretch all the
way into the clouds.

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:40.400
Visually, these are some very striking scenes,
but they might have originally been planned

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.930
as an easter egg to Disney's animated movie
Gigantic, an adaptation of Jack and the Beanstalk,

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:50.349
which was due to release the same year as
Onward until it was cancelled.

00:03:50.349 --> 00:03:54.519
Tom Holland and Chris Pratt are the voices
of Ian and Barley in Onward, but as actors

00:03:54.519 --> 00:03:58.659
they're both particularly well known for their
portrayals of Spider-Man and Star-Lord in

00:03:58.659 --> 00:04:00.700
the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:04.920
And so it's inevitable that Pixar squeezed
numerous Marvel references and easter eggs

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:06.029
into the movie.

00:04:06.029 --> 00:04:10.670
First of all, on Barley's jacket there's an
infinity gauntlet much like the one famously

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:13.680
wielded by Thanos in Marvel's Infinity Saga.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:17.940
There's also a sticker with the same design
plus the word "Gauntlet" underneath it on

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:21.049
the back of the passenger seat inside Barley's
van.

00:04:21.049 --> 00:04:25.770
The tiny red skull pin on Barley's jacket
could be a nod to the Marvel villain Red Skull

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:29.140
who becomes the guardian of the Soul Stone
in the Marvelverse.

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:33.840
Before this Red Skull was the head of HYDRA
in the MCU, and there's a potential nod to

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.039
that organisation via the Hydra badge on Barley's
jacket.

00:04:37.039 --> 00:04:38.039
"Hail Hydra."

00:04:38.039 --> 00:04:42.060
Though, of course, hydra is also a deadly
multi-headed serpent from Greek and Roman

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.700
mythology, and a Dungeons and Dragons monster.

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:47.520
More on D&amp;D easter eggs later.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:51.449
And the Phoenix Gem which brings the brother's
father back to life is orange-coloured, just

00:04:51.449 --> 00:04:52.449
like the MCU's Soul Stone.

00:04:52.449 --> 00:04:53.809
And the Phoenix Gem and the Soul Stone have
some overlapping magical properties.

00:04:53.809 --> 00:04:58.139
In Onward, the gem gives Ian and Barley a
day to interact with their dead father,

00:04:58.139 --> 00:05:02.650
and in Avengers: Infinity War, the Soul Stone
temporarily allowed Thanos to communicate

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:05.860
with his dead daughter Gamora when he was
in the Soulworld.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:10.080
And Barley, who's voiced by Chris Pratt, shares
some similar characteristics with Pratt's

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.530
MCU character Star-Lord, aka Peter Quill.

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:17.620
Both characters have a love mix tapes and
both of them had a traumatic experience as

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:20.180
a child when one of their parents died.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:23.810
In Onward, Barley regrets that he didn't go
in to say goodbye to his father when he was

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:27.110
ill because he was scared of all the tubes
and how unwell he was.

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:31.570
And when his mother was about to die in Guardians
of the Galaxy, as a child Peter Quill couldn't

00:05:31.570 --> 00:05:35.560
bear to hold her hand or look at her, something
that he would also later regret.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:40.331
Ian, who's voiced by Tom Holland, wears red
and blue colours, just like Spider-Man who

00:05:40.331 --> 00:05:45.920
he plays in the MCU, and when Ian says he'll
do "whatever it takes" to meet his dad, it

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:47.699
echoes a famous line from Avengers: Endgame.

00:05:47.699 --> 00:05:50.310
"This is the fight of our lives.

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:51.449
Whatever it takes."

00:05:51.449 --> 00:05:55.500
Onward also includes many hat tips to other
fantasy pop culture and movies.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:59.509
When Ian visits the fast food restaurant,
there's a sign for "second breakfast", a nod

00:05:59.509 --> 00:06:00.509
to J.R.R.

00:06:00.509 --> 00:06:04.789
Tolkien's The Hobbit in which Bilbo Baggins
likes to eat a second breakfast, something

00:06:04.789 --> 00:06:08.120
also true of the hobbits in Peter Jackson's
Lord of the Rings trilogy.

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:09.150
"What about breakfast?"

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:10.520
"You've already had it."

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:11.889
"We've had one, yes.

00:06:11.889 --> 00:06:13.360
What about second breakfast?"

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:19.080
And the LOR on Barley's "Quest Mix" tape is
possibly another nod to Lord of the Rings.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:24.740
Barley later plays a special mix tape titled
"Rise to Valhalla" when he sends his beloved

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:27.199
van Guinevere off on her final mission.

00:06:27.199 --> 00:06:32.690
Valhalla is a sacred place in Norse mythology
where many fallen warriors go to after death.

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:37.099
The details in this scene are perfect with
Barley's parking tickets fluttering out of

00:06:37.099 --> 00:06:41.610
the windows giving the sense that Guinevere
has wings, and the sound of the van's tyres

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:46.470
on the cobbles creates a galloping sound as
if she's riding into battle.

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:49.949
Dungeons and Dragons is also a huge inspiration
for Onward.

00:06:49.949 --> 00:06:54.410
There's Barley's "Quest of Yore" role-playing
game complete with a guide that resembles

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:57.659
a D&amp;D manual with spell-casting instructions.

00:06:57.659 --> 00:07:03.020
And the cover of the book cleverly foreshadows
an important moment later in the movie.

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:08.569
On Barley's jacket, there's a d20 dice, which
for anyone unfamiliar is a 20-sided dice used

00:07:08.569 --> 00:07:09.860
in Dungeons and Dragons.

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:15.229
And Barley also has a d20 dice stitching with
lightning bolts on his jacket sleeve.

00:07:15.229 --> 00:07:19.460
The Gelatinous Cube, which Ian and Barley
encounter after they venture into some caves,

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:23.130
is a classic D&amp;D monster taken directly from
the game.

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:28.449
The oozing cube is an antagonist that can
absorb and digest the victims it traps.

00:07:28.449 --> 00:07:32.629
And the many other murderous traps in the
cave's corridors give this scene an Indiana

00:07:32.629 --> 00:07:36.599
Jones vibe, together with the way that Ian
and Barley pull their father's legs through

00:07:36.599 --> 00:07:41.220
the closing stone door just in the nick of
time, much like Indy's famous hat grab in

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:43.159
The Temple of Doom.

00:07:43.159 --> 00:07:47.770
Another 80s reference comes at the end of
Onward when instead of driving off in Barley's

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:52.900
van along the road, the van flies away with
the power of magic, mimicking the end of Back

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:57.360
to the Future when Doc returns with his time
machine car from the future and then he, Marty

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:00.340
and Jennifer fly away instead of driving down
the road.

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:01.340
"Roads?

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:04.449
Where we're going, we don't need roads."

00:08:04.449 --> 00:08:08.710
And that Back to the Future reference seems
especially fitting given the recent deep fake

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:11.569
of Tom Holland as Marty McFly.

00:08:11.569 --> 00:08:15.283
As always in every Pixar film, there's a cameo
from John Ratzenberger who this time plays

00:08:15.283 --> 00:08:17.979
a character called Fenwick who's one of the
construction workers that yells at Barley

00:08:17.979 --> 00:08:21.300
when he's getting in the way of the demolition
of the stone fountain.

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:25.819
Towards the end of the movie, Officer Bronco
is called in for duty with the police reporting

00:08:25.819 --> 00:08:28.849
that there's an "A113 in progress".

00:08:28.849 --> 00:08:33.620
A113 is a regular Pixar easter egg that's
a tribute to the California Institute of the

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:37.070
Arts classroom where many of Pixar's animators
studied.

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:41.840
In Ian and Barley's kitchen, there's a nod
to Disney's Sleeping Beauty and Maleficent

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:44.890
with an "Aurora" branded washing-up liquid!

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:48.820
And on one of the shelves in Ian's room, there's
a wooden dinosaur figure that resembles the

00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:52.860
mysterious Pet Collector from Pixar's The
Good Dinosaur.

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:57.880
Onward is also chock-full of sign and brand
gags, great puns and other pop culture references.

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:02.060
In the opening sequence, there's a scene in
an arcade where a centaur is playing a game

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:06.940
called "Prance Prance Revolution", a play
on the music video game "Dance Dance Revolution".

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:12.480
At home, the boys' mother, Laurel, uses "Whey
Strong" chocolate flavour protein powder for

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:16.960
her smoothies and Ian eats Trollios cereal,
a likely pun on Cheerios.

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:21.280
Other brand name gags, include "Cloak &amp; Cola",
clearly Coca Cola;

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:25.970
Mountain Doom, Onward's version of Mountain
Dew which is also a nod to Lord of the Rings

00:09:25.970 --> 00:09:29.270
Mount Doom;
and Satyrade, a pun on Gatorade.

00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:33.700
It's also funny that the troll at the toll
booth works for the "Under Bridge Transportation

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:38.560
Authority", a joke about how trolls in some
fairy tales like "Three Billy Goats Gruffs"

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:41.820
wait underneath bridges to prey on those who
try to cross.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:47.010
And it made me chuckle when I noticed you
can either pay the toll with cash or a riddle.

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:50.450
Now, what other easter eggs or cool details
did you spot in Onward?

00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:54.600
And do you have any theories on where the
movie fits into the Pixar universe?

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:56.110
Leave your thoughts in the comments below.

00:09:56.110 --> 00:10:01.250
If you enjoyed this, don't forget to leave
a like and subscribe for all my new videos.

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:05.350
Tap left for another Pixar video or tap right
for something you're sure to like.

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:07.450
Thanks for watching and see ya next time.

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:08.740
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

